80
00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:30.880
[George] Even his closest newspaper editors abandoned him in the face of the administration's fierce repudiations.

81
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:38.640
[George] Less than a year after his secret surgery, Cleveland faced a domestic crisis that threatened to plunge the nation into civil war.

82
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.640
[George] In the model company town of Pullman, Illinois, south of Chicago, railcar tycoon George M.

83
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.680
[George] Pullman operated a feudal corporate empire.

84
00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:59.920
[George] In the wake of the panic of 1893, Pullman slashed his workers' wages by 25% while refusing to reduce the rents on company-owned housing or the prices in company-owned grocery stores.

85
00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:11.520
[George] Desperate workers went on strike in May 1894, appealing for help to the American Railway Union, led by the charismatic, radical labor organizer Eugene Victor Debs.

86
00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:18.400
[George] Backslash and backslash and Debs called for a nationwide boycott of all trains carrying Pullman sleeping cars.

87
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:25.120
[George] Within days, 150,000 railroad workers walked off the job across 27 states.

88
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.120
[George] The entire railroad network west of Chicago was paralyzed.

89
00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] Freight yards were blockaded, switches were spiked, and passenger trains were halted by large, angry crowds.

90
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] In Chicago, desperate mobs of unemployed drifters torched hundreds of railcars, sending massive plumes of black smoke into the sky and cutting off food supplies to eastern cities.

91
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:54.480
[George] Backslash and backslash and the Pullman strike exposed the stark, feudal reality of corporate company towns.

92
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.640
[George] Where workers were subjected to total economic control by industrial monopolies.

93
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:06.200
[George] Families faced immediate eviction from company homes if they dared to question wage deductions or management decrees.

94
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:17.000
[George] Illinois Governor John Peter Altgeld, a progressive Democrat, refused to request federal troops, insisting that local police and state militia could maintain order.

95
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.440
[George] But Grover Cleveland took a dramatically different view.

96
00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:22.520
[George] Cleveland's Attorney General.

97
00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:31.240
