110
00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:47.600
[George] Backslash and backslash and Cleveland's deployment of federal troops over the fierce objections of the state governor established an enduring precedent for federal supremacy in national emergencies.

111
00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:55.440
[George] The Supreme Court later upheld the injunction in Enray Debs, affirming the broad inherent power of the executive branch.

112
00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:04.080
[George] Grover Cleveland's actions in the Pullman strike solidified his reputation as a leader of uncompromising, almost ruthless firmness.

113
00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.520
[George] Conservatives praised him for maintaining law, order, and constitutional supremacy.

114
00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:15.840
[George] Labor unions and working class Americans never forgave him, viewing him as a tool of corporate capital.

115
00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.640
[George] Yet Cleveland remained serenely indifferent to both praise and condemnation.

116
00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:31.280
[George] He believed that the duty of the chief executive was not to negotiate with lawlessness or yield to popular pressure, but to enforce the laws of the United States without hesitation.

117
00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:37.760
[George] Backslash and backslash and from the rocking saloon of the Yadonaida to the smoke-filled rail yards of Chicago.

118
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:43.760
[George] Grover Cleveland demonstrated an iron will that was unique among 19th-century presidents.

119
00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:49.360
[George] He was the anchor of the American state during its darkest hours of industrial transformation.

120
00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:54.480
